  "notes": "The duo that made honky-tonk a stadium format. The uptempo signature the entry names is a two-beat \"with a hard backbeat and quarter-note kick,\" so the kick anchors sit on all four beats and the velocity tiers are pushed above the genre's; the ballads are \"cross-stick with the snare entering only at the chorus.\" Harmony is the stated vocabulary — \"I–IV–V plus vi–IV–I–V on the ballads and a I–♭VII–IV rock borrow on the uptempo cuts\" — and the mixolydian weighting exists only to make that ♭VII singable. The bass rule is quoted: \"the bass doubles the kick on beat 1 of every bar as a rule,\" which is a high `followRootsProb` with almost no anticipation rather than a kick mirror. The countermelody is the entry's twin-lead: \"Telecaster and pedal steel trading 2-bar answers, fiddle doubling the steel a third above on choruses.\" ⚠ Boundary flag carried from the research: \"Boot Scootin' Boogie\" is a boogie/train-adjacent shuffle and \"Neon Moon\" is a slow swung 12/8 — both grooves belong to the sibling archetype genres. ⚠ Confidence is medium because the research rates it high-medium and marks the per-track key/tempo readings [LOW-CONF]; per-track harmonic detail is generalised there. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the melody register/density and the countermelody ratio — \"hard backbeat\" and \"chorus melodies are higher-register and longer-noted than verses\" are directions, and 110–127, 58–81 and 0.6 are this author's reading of them.",
